    id: 1, module: "Module 1: Security Fundamentals", title: "What is Cybersecurity?",
    type: "theory", duration: "25 min",
    content: `Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting computer systems, networks, programs, and data from digital attacks, unauthorized access, damage, and theft. In the modern era, virtually every aspect of human life — banking, healthcare, communication, commerce, government services — depends on digital infrastructure, making cybersecurity one of the most critical disciplines of our time.\n\nThe global cost of cybercrime reached $8 trillion in 2023 and is projected to exceed $10 trillion annually by 2025, making it the world's third-largest economy after the United States and China. In Africa, as internet penetration grows rapidly (from 2.1% in 2005 to over 43% in 2024), cybersecurity threats are escalating at a similar pace. Cameroon, like many developing nations, is increasingly becoming a target for cybercriminals exploiting gaps in digital literacy and security infrastructure.\n\nCybersecurity protects against threats at multiple layers:\n- Physical Layer: Server rooms, hardware devices, access cards\n- Network Layer: Firewalls, VPNs, encrypted communications\n- Application Layer: Secure coding, authentication, input validation\n- Data Layer: Encryption, access controls, data masking\n- Human Layer: User training, social engineering awareness, security policies\n\nKey concepts every cybersecurity professional must understand:\n\nVulnerability: A weakness in a system, software, or process that could be exploited. Example: An unpatched web server with a known CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) entry.\n\nThreat: Any potential danger that could exploit a vulnerability. Threats can be natural (power outages), unintentional (human error), or intentional (hackers, nation-states).\n\nRisk: The probability and impact of a threat exploiting a vulnerability. Risk = Threat × Vulnerability × Impact. Risk management involves identifying, assessing, and mitigating risks to acceptable levels.\n\nExploit: The actual method or code used to take advantage of a vulnerability. Zero-day exploits target vulnerabilities that are unknown to the vendor — extremely dangerous because no patch exists.\n\nAttack Vector: The path or means through which an attacker gains access. Common vectors include email phishing, vulnerable web applications, physical USB drives, and unpatched software.\n\nCybersecurity is a career field with significant talent shortage globally — an estimated 3.5 million unfilled positions worldwide. In Cameroon, government agencies, telecoms, banks, and NGOs all need cybersecurity professionals. This creates an exceptional opportunity for trained cybersecurity specialists.`,
