    id: 2, module: "Module 1: Security Fundamentals", title: "The CIA Triad",
    type: "theory", duration: "30 min",
    content: `The CIA Triad is the foundational model of information security. CIA stands for Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability — three core principles that every security control, policy, and technology aims to protect. Understanding the CIA Triad allows you to evaluate any security decision in a structured way.\n\nConfidentiality — ensuring that information is accessible only to those authorized to access it. Confidentiality is violated when unauthorized parties can read, copy, or view data.\n\nTechniques for ensuring confidentiality:\n- Encryption: Converting readable data into ciphertext. AES-256 encryption protects data at rest; TLS protects data in transit.\n- Access Controls: Authentication (verifying identity) and authorization (verifying permission). Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) gives users only the permissions they need for their role — the Principle of Least Privilege.\n- Data Classification: Categorizing data by sensitivity (Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted) and applying controls accordingly.\n- Secure Channels: VPNs, HTTPS, SSH tunnels for transmitting sensitive data.\n\nConfidentiality example attack: A data breach where a hacker steals a database of customer credit card numbers. The attacker gained access to confidential data they were not authorized to view.\n\nIntegrity — ensuring that data is accurate, complete, and has not been tampered with or altered by unauthorized parties. Integrity is violated when data is modified without authorization, whether maliciously or accidentally.\n\nTechniques for ensuring integrity:\n- Hash Functions: A cryptographic hash (SHA-256) of a file produces a unique fingerprint. If the file is modified, the hash changes — detecting tampering.\n- Digital Signatures: Combine hashing with asymmetric cryptography to verify both authenticity and integrity.\n- Checksums: Simpler integrity verification for network transmissions.\n- Version Control: Git tracks every change to code with commit hashes.\n- Audit Logs: Immutable records of who made what changes and when.\n\nIntegrity example attack: SQL injection modifying a university grade database to change failing scores to passing grades.\n\nAvailability — ensuring that systems and data are accessible to authorized users when needed. Availability is violated when legitimate users cannot access resources they need.\n\nTechniques for ensuring availability:\n- Redundancy: Multiple servers, power supplies, and internet connections so a single failure doesn't bring down the system.\n- Backups: Regular, tested backups stored separately (3-2-1 rule: 3 copies, 2 media types, 1 offsite).\n- DDoS Protection: Services like Cloudflare absorb volumetric attacks.\n- Disaster Recovery: Documented plans for restoring services after a catastrophic failure.\n\nAvailability example attack: A Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attack floods a bank's website with millions of requests, making it unavailable to real customers.\n\nThe Triad creates tensions: Maximum confidentiality (encrypting everything) can reduce availability. Strong integrity checks add processing overhead. Good security professionals balance these three properties according to the data's value and sensitivity.`,
