    id: 3, module: "Module 1: Security Fundamentals", title: "Types of Cyber Threats",
    type: "theory", duration: "30 min",
    content: `Understanding the landscape of cyber threats is essential for any security professional. Threats come in many forms — malicious software, social engineering, network attacks, insider threats, and nation-state espionage. Each requires different defenses.\n\nMalware (Malicious Software) — software designed to disrupt, damage, or gain unauthorized access:\n\nVirus: Self-replicating code that attaches to legitimate programs and spreads when those programs are executed. Early viruses spread via floppy disks; modern ones via email attachments and downloads.\n\nWorm: Unlike viruses, worms spread independently across networks without user interaction. The WannaCry worm (2017) infected 230,000+ computers across 150 countries in a single day by exploiting an SMB vulnerability.\n\nTrojan Horse: Malware disguised as legitimate software. A user installs what appears to be a free game or utility, but it secretly installs malware, creates backdoors, or steals data.\n\nRansomware: Encrypts victim's files and demands payment (usually in cryptocurrency) for decryption keys. Healthcare systems, hospitals, and critical infrastructure are common targets because they cannot afford downtime. The Colonial Pipeline ransomware attack (2021) disrupted US fuel supplies for days.\n\nSpyware: Silently collects information from the victim — keystrokes, screenshots, browsing history, financial data — and sends it to attackers.\n\nAdware: Displays unwanted advertisements and may track user behaviour to serve targeted ads.\n\nRootkit: Provides hidden, privileged access to a system while concealing its presence. Extremely difficult to detect and remove — sometimes requires complete OS reinstallation.\n\nBotnet: A network of compromised computers (bots/zombies) controlled remotely by an attacker (botmaster). Used for DDoS attacks, spam campaigns, and cryptocurrency mining.\n\nSocial Engineering — exploiting human psychology rather than technical vulnerabilities:\n\nPhishing: Fraudulent emails or websites that mimic legitimate entities to steal credentials or install malware. Spear Phishing targets specific individuals with personalized content. Whaling targets high-value executives.\n\nVishing (Voice Phishing): Phone calls impersonating bank officers, tax authorities, or tech support to extract sensitive information.\n\nSmishing: SMS-based phishing attacks. Example: A message claiming your MTN Mobile Money account is suspended, containing a malicious link.\n\nBaiting: Leaving malware-infected USB drives in public places, hoping curious employees will plug them in.\n\nPretexting: Creating a fabricated scenario to manipulate a victim. Example: Impersonating an IT technician to gain access to a server room.\n\nNetwork Attacks:\nMan-in-the-Middle (MitM): Intercepting communications between two parties without their knowledge.\nDDoS: Overwhelming a target with traffic from a botnet.\nSQL Injection: Injecting malicious SQL into a web application's database queries.\nDNS Spoofing: Poisoning DNS records to redirect users to malicious websites.`,
