    id: 4, module: "Module 1: Security Fundamentals", title: "Threat Actors & Motivation",
    type: "theory", duration: "25 min",
    content: `Not all cyber attackers are the same. Understanding who attacks systems and why is critical for tailoring defenses appropriately. Threat actor profiling helps security teams prioritize resources — you defend differently against a bored teenager than against a nation-state with a $1 billion cyber budget.\n\nScript Kiddies — Low-skill attackers who use pre-written tools and scripts without understanding the underlying techniques. They typically target opportunistically, attacking any vulnerable system rather than specific targets. Despite low skill, they cause significant damage in aggregate and are the most common threat to poorly configured systems.\n\nHacktivists — Attackers motivated by ideological, political, or social causes. Anonymous and LulzSec are famous examples. They use DDoS attacks, website defacement, and data leaks against organizations whose values they oppose. Examples: attacks on government websites during political protests, data leaks exposing corporate corruption.\n\nCybercriminals — Financially motivated attackers. They conduct ransomware campaigns, business email compromise (BEC), credit card fraud, identity theft, and cryptocurrency theft. Organized cybercrime groups operate like businesses with technical staff, customer service, and even HR departments. Eastern European cybercrime groups like FIN7 and the Carbanak Group have stolen billions from financial institutions.\n\nInsider Threats — Employees, contractors, or partners who abuse legitimate access either maliciously or negligently. Particularly dangerous because they bypass perimeter defenses. Categories:\n- Malicious Insider: Deliberately stealing data, sabotaging systems\n- Negligent Insider: Accidentally clicking phishing links, misconfiguring systems\n- Compromised Insider: A legitimate user whose credentials were stolen\n\nNation-State Actors — Governments sponsoring offensive cyber operations for espionage, sabotage, and warfare. The most sophisticated and well-resourced threat actors. Notable groups:\n- APT28 (Fancy Bear) — Russian military intelligence (GRU)\n- Lazarus Group — North Korea (DPRK), responsible for $1.7 billion in cryptocurrency thefts\n- APT41 (Double Dragon) — China, conducts both espionage and financially motivated attacks\nNation-state attacks target critical infrastructure, government agencies, defense contractors, and intellectual property.\n\nAPT (Advanced Persistent Threat) — A sophisticated, long-term attack campaign typically by nation-states or well-resourced criminal groups. APTs infiltrate target networks and remain hidden for months or years, silently exfiltrating data rather than causing obvious disruption.\n\nCybersecurity Researchers / Ethical Hackers (White Hats) — Legitimate security professionals who find vulnerabilities and report them responsibly. Bug bounty programs pay researchers for discovering and disclosing vulnerabilities to vendors before criminals exploit them.\n\nAttribution Challenge: Accurately attributing cyberattacks to specific actors is extraordinarily difficult. Attackers use false flags, route through compromised third-party systems, and mimic other groups' techniques.`,
