    id: 13, module: "Module 2: Networking for Security", title: "IP Addressing & Subnetting",
    type: "theory", duration: "35 min",
    content: `IP addressing and subnetting are foundational networking concepts every security professional must master. Network segmentation through subnetting is a key defense strategy — isolating sensitive systems on separate subnets limits the blast radius of security breaches.\n\nIPv4 Addressing:\nIPv4 addresses are 32-bit numbers written in dotted decimal: 192.168.1.100\nEach number (octet) represents 8 bits: 0-255.\nTotal possible addresses: 2³² = 4,294,967,296 (~4.3 billion)\n\nAddress Classes (historical, now replaced by CIDR):\n- Class A: 1.0.0.0 to 126.255.255.255 — /8 default mask\n- Class B: 128.0.0.0 to 191.255.255.255 — /16 default mask\n- Class C: 192.0.0.0 to 223.255.255.255 — /24 default mask\n\nPrivate IP Ranges (RFC 1918 — not routable on public internet):\n- 10.0.0.0/8 (Class A private)\n- 172.16.0.0/12 (Class B private)\n- 192.168.0.0/16 (Class C private)\n\nSubnet Masks and CIDR:\nA subnet mask defines which portion of an IP is the network and which is the host.\n255.255.255.0 = /24 = 11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000\nThe /24 means the first 24 bits identify the network.\n\nCalculating subnet details for 192.168.1.0/24:\n- Network address: 192.168.1.0 (first address — not usable)\n- Broadcast address: 192.168.1.255 (last address — not usable)\n- Usable hosts: 2⁸ - 2 = 254 (addresses .1 through .254)\n\nCIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing):\nAllows flexible prefix lengths, not just /8, /16, /24:\n- /25 = 128 addresses (126 usable)\n- /26 = 64 addresses (62 usable)\n- /27 = 32 addresses (30 usable)\n- /30 = 4 addresses (2 usable — for point-to-point links)\n- /32 = 1 address (a single host — used in firewall rules)\n\nSecurity Applications of Subnetting:\n\nNetwork Segmentation: Dividing a network into isolated subnets reduces lateral movement risk:\n- DMZ subnet: 192.168.10.0/24 (web servers, mail servers)\n- Internal subnet: 10.0.1.0/24 (employee workstations)\n- Server subnet: 10.0.2.0/24 (databases, internal applications)\n- Management subnet: 10.0.100.0/24 (network devices, admin access)\n\nFirewall rules enforce which subnets can communicate:\n- DMZ → Internet: Allow (web servers need internet)\n- Internet → DMZ: Controlled (only ports 80, 443)\n- DMZ → Internal: DENY (critical security boundary)\n- Internal → Server: Allow only specific application ports\n\nNAT (Network Address Translation): Translates private IPs to public IPs, hiding internal addressing from the internet. PAT (Port Address Translation/NAT Overload) allows many devices to share one public IP using different ports.\n\nSpecial Addresses:\n- 127.0.0.1 (Loopback/localhost): Always refers to the local machine\n- 169.254.0.0/16 (APIPA): Assigned when DHCP fails — indicates connectivity problems\n- 0.0.0.0: Default route (all networks)`,
